 "pricesNote": "Per person, cruise only, USD, two sharing, as published in Galaxy Cruises' online booking feed on 1 Nov 2026. Every cabin is priced the same; single occupancy is 1.5 times the sharing rate. 2026: Northern US$4,600, Western and Southern US$5,700. 2027: Northern US$5,200, Western and Southern US$6,500. Christmas and New Year departures cost more (up to US$9,750 in 2027). Not included: international and Galapagos flights, the Galapagos National Park entrance fee (US$200 per person), the US$20 Transit Control Card, alcoholic drinks, tips, personal expenses and insurance.",
 "inclusions": [
  "Cabin as booked, all meals from lunch on day one to breakfast on the last day",
  "A snack after each activity (except the last day) and a welcome cocktail",
  "Soft drinks, purified water, coffee and tea",
  "All visits, excursions and activities in the itinerary",
  "Bilingual (English and Spanish) naturalist guiding and an onboard cruise director",
  "Airport assistance on the day the cruise starts, and transport in the Galapagos Marine Reserve",
  "Use of docks and ferries",
  "Snorkelling gear (mask, snorkel, fins), wetsuits, kayaks and paddleboards in designated areas",
  "Airport to canal and canal to airport transport (Lobitos bus)",
  "Wi-Fi (Starlink)"
 ],
 "exclusions": [
  "International flights to and from Ecuador",
  "Flights between Quito or Guayaquil and the Galapagos",
  "Galapagos National Park entrance fee",
  "Transit Control Card (INGALA), US$20 per person",
  "Alcoholic drinks",
  "Tips for guides and crew",
  "Personal expenses and travel insurance"
 ],
 "children": "Galaxy Cruises accepts children over 6 on shared departures; on charters, children of any age sail. Children under 12 must share a cabin with an adult, or be in an adjacent cabin to their parents or guardians.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "How many guests does the Alya catamaran take?",
   "Sixteen, in nine double or twin cabins: five on the Main Deck and four on the Upper Deck. There are nine crew, a cruise director and one bilingual naturalist guide."
  ],
  [
   "Which Alya itinerary should I choose?",
   "The Northern Route (4 nights) goes to Genovesa, Santiago, Bartolome and Chinese Hat. The Western Route (5 nights) covers Isabela and Fernandina. The Southern Route (5 nights) covers Espanola, Floreana, San Cristobal and the central islets. All start at Baltra, so two can be joined back to back."
  ],
  [
   "How much does an Alya cruise cost?",
   "Galaxy's 2027 rates are US$5,200 per person for the 4-night Northern Route and US$6,500 for the 5-night Western or Southern Route, two sharing. Single occupancy is 1.5 times that. Flights, the park fee and the transit card are extra."
  ],
  [
   "What is included on Alya?",
   "All meals, snacks, soft drinks, water, coffee and tea, guided excursions, snorkelling gear, wetsuits, kayaks and paddleboards, and airport assistance on the first day. Alcohol, tips, flights and park fees are not included."
  ],
  [
   "Is there Wi-Fi on Alya?",
   "Yes, Starlink. Galaxy notes the satellite connection can be intermittent around the islands."
  ],
  [
   "Can children sail on Alya?",
   "Children over 6 can join shared departures, and children of any age on a full charter. Under-12s share a cabin with an adult or sleep in a cabin next to their parents."
  ],
  [
   "Will I get seasick on a catamaran?",
   "Galaxy says Alya's twin hull is much more stable than a single-hull yacht. If you are prone to motion sickness, bring medication."
  ],
  [
   "What luggage can I bring on Alya?",
   "Galaxy recommends one soft-sided suitcase of up to 23 kg (50 lb) and one carry-on, because of cabin storage and panga transfers."
  ]
 ],
